      "text": "Energy as a Foundation for Economic Growth",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Energy as a Foundation for Economic Growth",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "q8e4J7qDDO8XkPOXXHzrg",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 261.44567016348697,
      "y": 531,
      "width": 755.1715258839636,
      "height": 2077.669542634449,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 738942610,
      "version": 784,
      "versionNonce": 1608661454,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1794925594802,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The chart showing the relationship between energy consumption and GDP\nacross countries shows a strong positive correlation. It shows that as a \ncountry's energy use per capita (y-axis) increases, its GDP per capita \n(x-axis) rises proportionally. Energy acts as the physical engine for industria\nlization, manufacturing, and modern services.\n\nThe Global Divide:\n- High-Income Economies (Top-Right): Countries like the United States and \nCanada consume ~7 tonnes of oil equivalent (toe) per capita and generate \nthe highest GDP per capita (~$40k–$50k).   \n- Developing/Emerging Economies \n(Bottom-Left): Most African nations and India cluster below 1 toe per \ncapita and under $10k GDP per capita which is well below the world average \n(~1.9 toe). China sits right around the world average threshold.   \n\nThe chart basically shows that you can't build a rich economy without using a \nlot of energy. No country has high income (GDP per capita) while consuming low \nenergy. Developing countries like Nigeria, India, and most of Africa sit in the \nbottom-left with low energy use and low income, well below the world average. \nMeanwhile, countries like the US and Canada sit at the top-right with massive \nenergy use and high wealth.   \n\nHowever, look at Germany, Japan, and the EU: they make almost the same \namount of money per person as the US and Canada, but they burn roughly half \nthe energy to do it. Why? Because they have better energy efficiency, milder \nclimates, compact cities with great public transit, and economies focused on \nhigh-value tech and services rather than heavy, energy-guzzling industries. \n\nIn short, energy drives wealth, but efficiency determines how much wealth you \nsqueeze out of every unit of energy.\n\n\n                      Energy as a Foundation for human development\n\n• Access to energy, of which electricity is an important form, correlates closely \n  with human development\n• This also affect the country Human Development Index (HDI).\n• HDI is a commonly use metric that attempt to measure development or \n  well-being of a country, accounting for income, health and education.\n• The higher HDI scores are associated with more developed counties.\n• Countries with high HDI scores tend to use more energy per person\n   than those with lower HDI scores. \n\nThe chart showing the total primary energy use and HDI of some countries in \nthe world implies some things that neds to be taken into consideration and \nunderstood.\n\nRelationship Between Primary Energy Use and Human Development Index (HDI):\n- Positive Correlation: The chart demonstrates that higher per-capita primary \n  energy consumption directly supports higher Human Development Index (HDI) \n  scores, encompassing life expectancy, education, and living standards. \n  Countries with severe energy poverty (such as Niger Republic and Nigeria) \n  exhibit the lowest HDI levels.  \n- Diminishing Marginal Returns (Saturation Effect): While increasing energy access \n  from low to moderate levels produces rapid gains in human development (seen \n  moving from Nigeria and India to Spain and Japan), HDI plateaus around \n  0.85–0.95 once basic and industrial needs are met. For instance, Spain and \n  Japan achieve nearly the same high HDI (~0.86–0.90) as the United States \n  (~0.90) despite consuming less than half the energy per capita, proving that \n  human well-being does not require boundless energy consumption once efficiency \n  and essential services are secured.\n\n                                       Units of Energy \n\nENERGY (E) vs. POWER (P)\n• Energy (E): Total work done / total capacity [Unit: Joule (J)]\n  - Mechanical: 1 J = 1 N·m\n  - Fluid: 1 J = 1 Pa·m³\n  - Electrical: 1 J = 1 W·s\n• Power (P): Rate of energy transfer (P = dE/dt) [Unit: Watt (W)]\n• Mental Model: Water Tank → Energy = Total water in tank; Power = Tap flow \n  rate.\n\nWHY DIFFERENT UNITS? (1 Joule is tiny, so industries use bigger \"buckets\")\n• Electrical Grid:\n  - 1 Wh = 3,600 J\n  - 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J (3.6 MJ) → Home meters\n  - 1 MWh = 3.6 × 10⁹ J → Power plants\n  - 1 TWh = 3.6 × 10¹⁵ J → National grids\n• Thermal / HVAC:\n  - 1 cal = 4.1868 J | 1 kcal = 4186.8 J\n  - 1 BTU = 1055.87 J → Air conditioners\n• Global Oil & National Policy:\n  - 1 koe = 41.868 × 10⁶ J\n  - 1 toe = 41.868 × 10⁹ J (41.868 GJ) → Global energy reports\n  - 1 Quad = 10¹⁵ BTU = 1055.87 × 10¹⁵ J\n\n",
      "fontSize": 18.887904933040442,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The chart showing the relationship between energy consumption and GDP\nacross countries shows a strong positive correlation. It shows that as a \ncountry's energy use per capita (y-axis) increases, its GDP per capita \n(x-axis) rises proportionally. Energy acts as the physical engine for industria\nlization, manufacturing, and modern services.\n\nThe Global Divide:\n- High-Income Economies (Top-Right): Countries like the United States and \nCanada consume ~7 tonnes of oil equivalent (toe) per capita and generate \nthe highest GDP per capita (~$40k–$50k).   \n- Developing/Emerging Economies \n(Bottom-Left): Most African nations and India cluster below 1 toe per \ncapita and under $10k GDP per capita which is well below the world average \n(~1.9 toe). China sits right around the world average threshold.   \n\nThe chart basically shows that you can't build a rich economy without using a \nlot of energy. No country has high income (GDP per capita) while consuming low \nenergy. Developing countries like Nigeria, India, and most of Africa sit in the \nbottom-left with low energy use and low income, well below the world average. \nMeanwhile, countries like the US and Canada sit at the top-right with massive \nenergy use and high wealth.   \n\nHowever, look at Germany, Japan, and the EU: they make almost the same \namount of money per person as the US and Canada, but they burn roughly half \nthe energy to do it. Why? Because they have better energy efficiency, milder \nclimates, compact cities with great public transit, and economies focused on \nhigh-value tech and services rather than heavy, energy-guzzling industries. \n\nIn short, energy drives wealth, but efficiency determines how much wealth you \nsqueeze out of every unit of energy.\n\n\n                      Energy as a Foundation for human development\n\n• Access to energy, of which electricity is an important form, correlates closely \n  with human development\n• This also affect the country Human Development Index (HDI).\n• HDI is a commonly use metric that attempt to measure development or \n  well-being of a country, accounting for income, health and education.\n• The higher HDI scores are associated with more developed counties.\n• Countries with high HDI scores tend to use more energy per person\n   than those with lower HDI scores. \n\nThe chart showing the total primary energy use and HDI of some countries in \nthe world implies some things that neds to be taken into consideration and \nunderstood.\n\nRelationship Between Primary Energy Use and Human Development Index (HDI):\n- Positive Correlation: The chart demonstrates that higher per-capita primary \n  energy consumption directly supports higher Human Development Index (HDI) \n  scores, encompassing life expectancy, education, and living standards. \n  Countries with severe energy poverty (such as Niger Republic and Nigeria) \n  exhibit the lowest HDI levels.  \n- Diminishing Marginal Returns (Saturation Effect): While increasing energy access \n  from low to moderate levels produces rapid gains in human development (seen \n  moving from Nigeria and India to Spain and Japan), HDI plateaus around \n  0.85–0.95 once basic and industrial needs are met. For instance, Spain and \n  Japan achieve nearly the same high HDI (~0.86–0.90) as the United States \n  (~0.90) despite consuming less than half the energy per capita, proving that \n  human well-being does not require boundless energy consumption once efficiency \n  and essential services are secured.\n\n                                       Units of Energy \n\nENERGY (E) vs. POWER (P)\n• Energy (E): Total work done / total capacity [Unit: Joule (J)]\n  - Mechanical: 1 J = 1 N·m\n  - Fluid: 1 J = 1 Pa·m³\n  - Electrical: 1 J = 1 W·s\n• Power (P): Rate of energy transfer (P = dE/dt) [Unit: Watt (W)]\n• Mental Model: Water Tank → Energy = Total water in tank; Power = Tap flow \n  rate.\n\nWHY DIFFERENT UNITS? (1 Joule is tiny, so industries use bigger \"buckets\")\n• Electrical Grid:\n  - 1 Wh = 3,600 J\n  - 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J (3.6 MJ) → Home meters\n  - 1 MWh = 3.6 × 10⁹ J → Power plants\n  - 1 TWh = 3.6 × 10¹⁵ J → National grids\n• Thermal / HVAC:\n  - 1 cal = 4.1868 J | 1 kcal = 4186.8 J\n  - 1 BTU = 1055.87 J → Air conditioners\n• Global Oil & National Policy:\n  - 1 koe = 41.868 × 10⁶ J\n  - 1 toe = 41.868 × 10⁹ J (41.868 GJ) → Global energy reports\n  - 1 Quad = 10¹⁵ BTU = 1055.87 × 10¹⁵ J\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
