      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1105048978,
      "version": 103,
      "versionNonce": 763965774,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1794925626839,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "                Global Energy Assessments/ World Energy System\n• The global energy system is a complex network of energy\nsources, bulk transportation, storage, distribution, and end use\n• The energy system is constantly evolving to meet growing\ndemand and responding to economic, social, and\nenvironmental constraints.\n• The existing world energy system does not adequately meet\nthe needs of all people, nor is it sustainable in the long-term.\n• Forty percent (40%) of the world lives in energy poverty.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                Global Energy Assessments/ World Energy System\n• The global energy system is a complex network of energy\nsources, bulk transportation, storage, distribution, and end use\n• The energy system is constantly evolving to meet growing\ndemand and responding to economic, social, and\nenvironmental constraints.\n• The existing world energy system does not adequately meet\nthe needs of all people, nor is it sustainable in the long-term.\n• Forty percent (40%) of the world lives in energy poverty.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "isiieY1swF7WlxpcO4dHo",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 255,
      "y": 2880.6666107177734,
      "width": 793.1793823242188,
      "height": 1725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aA",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 546883474,
      "version": 430,
      "versionNonce": 1112458130,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1794931231874,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "                            World Energy Consumption\n• The present global yearly primary energy consumption (i.e\nthe gross energy before its transformation into other more\nuseful forms like electricity) is about 500 × 10^18J,\nequivalent to about 1.4 × 10^17Wh or 140,000 TWh\n• As the rate of energy consumption is increasing all over the\nworld, the supply (coal, petroleum, gas- fossil fuel) is\ndepleting, hence resulting in inflation and energy shortage.\n• This is called “energy crisis”.\n\n                             Increasing Energy Demand\n* Global Energy Demand is expected to rise by about 40% in 2040.\n- Throughout history, global per capital consumption of energy has trended \n  upward, with the world average annual energy consumption increasing each year.\n- Growth in urbanisation and changing population framework lead to an \n  increase in the demand for energy access and infrastructure.\n\n                        Challenges of Increasing Energy Demand\n• Presently, over 80% of the worldwide energy is supplied by fossil fuels, which \n  are depletable and whose carbon dioxide emissions contribute to global \n  warming.\n• Fossil-fuel resources tend to be concentrated in certain regions.\n• Countries without natural supplies of fossil fuels must import energy.\n• This can pose energy supply challenges in developing countries with weak \n  currencies, poor credit, and without adequate infrastructure such as ports, \n  pipelines, and railways.\n\n\n                        Energy Inequality and disparity of use\n• However, there is vast inequality in energy consumption.\n• Per person consumption is higher in developed countries than the developing \n  ones.\n• Another disturbing aspect is the disparity in consumption between rich and \n  poor countries: the richest billion people of the planet consume over 50% of \n  all energy, while the poorest billion consume around 4%.\n• The per person annual consumption in Canada, for example, is 318 GJ, while \n  the average annual per person consumption in sub-Saharan Africa is just 29 GJ.\n• Also, the Per person consumption is higher in some countries in Northern and \n  Southern Africa than other countries in Africa\n\n\n                                    Energy Crisis\n• Energy crisis is any significant bottleneck (or price rises) in the supply of \n  energy resources to an economy.\n• It is usually referred to as the shortage of oil and additionally to electricity \n  or other natural resources.\n• An energy crisis may be referred to as an oil crisis, petroleum crisis, energy \n  storage, electricity shortage or electricity crisis. \n\n\n                                Causes of Energy Crisis\n1) Increasing Energy Usage or Over-Consumption\n2) Uneven Distribution of Energy Resources/ Energy Inequality\n3) Industrial actions (such as strike and government embargoes)\n4)Aging infrastructures\n5) Pipeline failure\n6) Damage from severe storm\n7) Lacks of Technical Knowhow\n8)Attack by terrorist or militant on government infrastructure\n9) Political upsurge (e.g change in government, regime change etc)\n\n\nFor easy remembrance, the causes of energy crises can be classified as thus:\n- Demand & Geography (#1–2): Too much consumption, unequal resources.   \n- Technical & Physical Failures (#4–7): Old grids, broken pipelines, storms, lack \n  of skilled engineers.   \n- Human & Political Disruptions (#3, #8–9): Strikes/embargoes, sabotage/van-\n  dalism, political instability.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                            World Energy Consumption\n• The present global yearly primary energy consumption (i.e\nthe gross energy before its transformation into other more\nuseful forms like electricity) is about 500 × 10^18J,\nequivalent to about 1.4 × 10^17Wh or 140,000 TWh\n• As the rate of energy consumption is increasing all over the\nworld, the supply (coal, petroleum, gas- fossil fuel) is\ndepleting, hence resulting in inflation and energy shortage.\n• This is called “energy crisis”.\n\n                             Increasing Energy Demand\n* Global Energy Demand is expected to rise by about 40% in 2040.\n- Throughout history, global per capital consumption of energy has trended \n  upward, with the world average annual energy consumption increasing each year.\n- Growth in urbanisation and changing population framework lead to an \n  increase in the demand for energy access and infrastructure.\n\n                        Challenges of Increasing Energy Demand\n• Presently, over 80% of the worldwide energy is supplied by fossil fuels, which \n  are depletable and whose carbon dioxide emissions contribute to global \n  warming.\n• Fossil-fuel resources tend to be concentrated in certain regions.\n• Countries without natural supplies of fossil fuels must import energy.\n• This can pose energy supply challenges in developing countries with weak \n  currencies, poor credit, and without adequate infrastructure such as ports, \n  pipelines, and railways.\n\n\n                        Energy Inequality and disparity of use\n• However, there is vast inequality in energy consumption.\n• Per person consumption is higher in developed countries than the developing \n  ones.\n• Another disturbing aspect is the disparity in consumption between rich and \n  poor countries: the richest billion people of the planet consume over 50% of \n  all energy, while the poorest billion consume around 4%.\n• The per person annual consumption in Canada, for example, is 318 GJ, while \n  the average annual per person consumption in sub-Saharan Africa is just 29 GJ.\n• Also, the Per person consumption is higher in some countries in Northern and \n  Southern Africa than other countries in Africa\n\n\n                                    Energy Crisis\n• Energy crisis is any significant bottleneck (or price rises) in the supply of \n  energy resources to an economy.\n• It is usually referred to as the shortage of oil and additionally to electricity \n  or other natural resources.\n• An energy crisis may be referred to as an oil crisis, petroleum crisis, energy \n  storage, electricity shortage or electricity crisis. \n\n\n                                Causes of Energy Crisis\n1) Increasing Energy Usage or Over-Consumption\n2) Uneven Distribution of Energy Resources/ Energy Inequality\n3) Industrial actions (such as strike and government embargoes)\n4)Aging infrastructures\n5) Pipeline failure\n6) Damage from severe storm\n7) Lacks of Technical Knowhow\n8)Attack by terrorist or militant on government infrastructure\n9) Political upsurge (e.g change in government, regime change etc)\n\n\nFor easy remembrance, the causes of energy crises can be classified as thus:\n- Demand & Geography (#1–2): Too much consumption, unequal resources.   \n- Technical & Physical Failures (#4–7): Old grids, broken pipelines, storms, lack \n  of skilled engineers.   \n- Human & Political Disruptions (#3, #8–9): Strikes/embargoes, sabotage/van-\n  dalism, political instability.\n",
